WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.295 --> 00:00:03.564
- Oh, my gosh, dude. Wow-
- What the hell is that, man?

00:00:03.564 --> 00:00:04.397
Look at a-

00:00:04.397 --> 00:00:05.280
- [Narrator] Government officials

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.740
can't answer this Naval pilot's question.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.860
With current data, they can
deduce just a few things

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.390
about this mystery dot.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.490
Based off of how high it is off the water

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.380
and how fast it's likely going,

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.390
it's probably drifting on the wind,

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.550
and it's brighter than the
ocean, it's floating above.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.380
So it's likely colder than the water

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.630
and not using a propulsion system.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.300
So it's probably not a spaceship.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.950
But because data is so limited,

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:33.690
it makes this a UAP, an
Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.100
- Identifying UAP is so important

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.500
because it's a matter
of national security.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.030
It's a matter of aviation safety

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.050
and it's incredibly important

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.240
that we are curious about
our world around us.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.350
- [Narrator] Here's why these objects

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.590
are so hard to identify and
how a new report from NASA

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.023
could help solve some of these mysteries.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.100
Ryan Graves is a former Naval F-18 pilot

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.210
who has been an advocate

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.090
for researching Unidentified
Anomalous Phenomena

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.060
or UAPs after witnessing one himself.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.250
- And that was very surreal to see that

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.190
because that we immediately
thought it was a radar error,

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.770
but it's almost more unsettling
when the thing you thought

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.020
was an error turned out
to be reality.(laughs)

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:10.853
- [Narrator] A new report

00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:13.410
from an Independent Study
Team appointed by NASA

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.760
is trying to make it easier

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.710
to identify some of these objects,

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.890
but the current data available
makes it a challenge.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.830
Lack of data was a problem
in identifying this UAP

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.480
in the Middle East.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.040
- While we are still looking at it,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.620
I don't have any more
data other than that.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.810
- [Narrator] One problem
is the quality of images.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.200
Right now, many are taken
from military aircraft.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.940
- It's often, you know,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.040
grainy footage where
it's kind of hard to tell

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.030
and it's like split-second
objects small spherical object

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.180
that might go across the
video extremely fast.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.460
- [Narrator] For example, look
at this one from South Asia.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.510
Officials determined

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.030
that this is likely just
a commercial aircraft,

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.850
but compression on the video
creates trails around it

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.943
and the quality makes it
hard to discern what it is.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.080
But it's difficult to get
higher-quality footage.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.570
Advanced radars like those from NOAA

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:07.050
and NASA aren't currently
watching for these objects.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.040
- There is not a mission

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.740
to go proactively look
for these right now.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.040
So what's happening is people
that are operating these tools

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.360
are seeing things that they can't explain.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.030
They have no way to report it,
but they're starting to now,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.340
and we're starting to see
that information come in.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.950
- [Narrator] The All-domain
Anomaly Resolution Office

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:27.180
or AARO heads up most efforts
to identify UAPs right now,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.370
but doesn't always have
access to the wealth of data

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.390
from places like NASA, the
FAA, and commercial agencies.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:34.320
- So this wasn't something

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.530
that people were looking
to develop these ways

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.200
of sharing information because
it wasn't on their radar.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.460
But now it is for agencies
like NASA and for the DOD

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.610
and so now, they're trying
to work more together,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.310
so that the government has
a way to look at this stuff

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.050
in one central place.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.520
- [Narrator] But one
of the biggest barriers

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.500
isn't a technological problem.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.180
It's a social one.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:02.180
- Do I believe there's life
in a universe that is so vast

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.920
that it's hard for me to comprehend

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.393
how big it is?

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.990
My personal answer is yes.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.150
- [Narrator] Talking openly about UAPs

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.630
or other unidentified objects
hasn't always been the norm.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.910
- Just because of what the perception

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.360
was in pop culture about UFOs.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.970
A lot of people felt like
if they were to come forward

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.200
and talk about what they would see,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.730
it would hurt them in certain ways.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.250
- [Narrator] Speculation about
aliens is often brought up

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.250
in conversations about identifying UAPs.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.970
- We need to move into the
realm of science on this

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.090
and make this a normal everyday
mundane scientific topic

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.880
that we can engage in
outside of controversy.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.110
- [Narrator] The NASA-appointed team,

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.020
says that none of the UAPs
they've identified so far,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.840
suggests that they have
extra terrestrial origins

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.170
and that should only be a
hypothesis of last resort.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.140
The report also says that
stigma around this topic

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.260
has prevented a lot of
data from being reported.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.420
The team concluded that NASA's involvement

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.720
could help lend credibility to
the issue and reduce stigma.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.990
The agency appointed a
director of UAP research,

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.000
a sign of its investment in the issue.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.320
- They're extremely well respected.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.470
They have a great mind trust at NASA

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.630
and they can apply a lot
of intellectual force

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:16.463
to this problem.

00:04:16.463 --> 00:04:18.990
So I think them coming into the game is,

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.570
it should be, I'll say, a
big win for transparency

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.131
and for better understanding this issue.

00:04:24.131 --> 00:04:26.714
(gentle music)

